  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH SATURDAY...Early afternoon satellite and radar\nimagery showed widespread clouds and rain across the region.\nHowever there was no lightning as instability remained to the\nsouth. The lack of lightning and deep convection meant rainfall\nrates were only about 0.1 to 0.2 inches per hour. This kept the\nflood threat low across most of the region.\n\nThe lone exception was down along I-90, where there is still a\nchance for some convection and heavier rainfall rates to develop\nthis evening. The flood threat has trended down, but did\ncontinue with the Flood Watch since there is a potential for\nstorms.\n\nOvernight, the steady rain will gradually end, but the low\nclouds will continue into Saturday. Forecast soundings showed\nlow level saturation and some omega, so did increase the chance\nfor precipitation Saturday morning, and went with drizzle\ninstead of rain. Winds will be out of the northeast at 10 to 15\nmph, and temperatures will be in the low 60s making it feel like\nfall.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY...Looking ahead, mostly dry conditions are\nexpected for next week. A boundary will lay out to the south\nacross Iowa, and be the focus for showers and storms. Meanwhile\nupper level ridging will lead to a surface high pressure\nbuilding across the Great Lakes. This will keep temperatures on\nthe cooler side to start the week. However, southerly winds\nstart to return by the end of next week, which will bring in\nslightly warmer air, and small chances for rain across the west.\nOverall expect dry weather this coming week.",
